WEBVTT

00:01.667 --> 00:05.280
I want to share what I've been able to do inside of Google

00:05.280 --> 00:11.000
Flow for coming up with like high quality,

00:11.000 --> 00:14.510
creative sort of brand imagery for

00:14.510 --> 00:18.080
this cleaning brand that I've been working

00:18.747 --> 00:22.850
on. I kind of found an unlock within

00:22.850 --> 00:26.480
Flow that allowed me to sort of

00:26.480 --> 00:30.800
make these high end kind of product

00:31.207 --> 00:35.060
shots that are, I think, up to par

00:35.060 --> 00:38.890
with some of the best creatives

00:38.890 --> 00:42.920
out there that you see on like these really successful brands.

00:42.920 --> 00:46.790
And how I've been able to do this is really quite

00:46.790 --> 00:50.440
simple. It all started with references.

00:50.440 --> 00:54.060
And so I kind of went and found a few examples of

00:54.060 --> 00:57.720
some product imagery or shots that I thought

00:57.720 --> 01:01.320
were really well done. These are

01:01.320 --> 01:04.840
two of the examples. And what I did is I fed those

01:04.840 --> 01:08.520
references into the agent that lives inside of Google

01:09.467 --> 01:13.080
Flow. This is a newer feature they added an agent

01:13.080 --> 01:16.680
before it was, I believe, all of it just kind

01:16.680 --> 01:20.540
of one off prompting you would get an output for every

01:20.540 --> 01:24.110
input you gave it. Now it's like you can do this back and forth with

01:24.110 --> 01:27.660
the agent. So I attached these two references

01:27.660 --> 01:31.210
as the sort of design direction and I asked

01:31.210 --> 01:34.800
quite simply how would you describe these

01:34.800 --> 01:38.960
from a lighting and composition point of view.

01:38.960 --> 01:42.550
And then I followed that up, same prompt and

01:42.550 --> 01:46.430
said I want to model this style of shot for X-All

01:46.430 --> 01:50.080
of the brand. And then the agent came back

01:50.080 --> 01:53.720
and basically said this is hard lighting.

01:53.720 --> 01:57.750
It's like studio quality frozen shot still

01:57.750 --> 02:01.460
type aesthetic. And then it asked what does

02:01.460 --> 02:05.240
X-All sell? And so I, in that same thread,

02:05.240 --> 02:09.130
I basically attached some reference images

02:09.130 --> 02:13.030
of actual X-All products. And I said here

02:13.030 --> 02:16.760
are the actual X-All products. You know,

02:16.760 --> 02:20.410
go wild I said, kind of more or less. And what it did was

02:20.410 --> 02:24.110
it started to generate these like action shots that

02:24.110 --> 02:27.870
you see now. Like it took our toilet cleaning foam

02:27.870 --> 02:31.640
powder and started to do some wild stuff with it. It

02:31.640 --> 02:35.460
even tried to turn the X-All logo into

02:35.460 --> 02:39.880
like a bubbly looking logo style abstract

02:39.880 --> 02:43.440
thing. I was not even expecting it.

02:43.440 --> 02:47.070
And it kept going with this, right? And it took all these real products

02:47.070 --> 02:50.810
that we sell, got the packaging right. You know, there

02:50.810 --> 02:55.080
were some outputs that weren't perfect, especially

02:55.080 --> 02:58.960
as I got into these more multi product shots. Like you'll

02:58.960 --> 03:02.600
notice it doesn't actually quite line up with what the

03:02.600 --> 03:06.500
label itself says. So there was some QA I had to do still,

03:06.500 --> 03:10.360
but for the most part, like, you know, these microfiber

03:10.360 --> 03:14.240
cleaning cloths look great. A lot of the stuff is really

03:14.240 --> 03:17.840
high end and high design, high quality. Colors

03:17.840 --> 03:21.630
might be a little off, but for the most part, I think it does a really

03:21.630 --> 03:25.230
great job. And a lot of these are just usable right

03:25.230 --> 03:29.280
out of the box. A lot of them might also require edits.

03:29.280 --> 03:32.830
And that's, of course, a part of the process. But for

03:32.830 --> 03:36.530
iterating and getting an actual design direction, you know,

03:36.530 --> 03:40.360
in place, it's pretty dang good.

03:40.360 --> 03:44.120
You know, it's like the tablet dissolving inside of the bottle looks

03:44.120 --> 03:48.090
great. So I wanted to just sort of share that and kind of showcase

03:48.090 --> 03:51.800
what's possible with Google Flow. If you

03:51.800 --> 03:55.840
find that, like, there's a brand that you're

03:55.840 --> 03:59.560
trying to grow, start with the visuals

03:59.560 --> 04:03.100
or, you know, if you're bored and just want to kind of see what it can

04:03.100 --> 04:06.650
do, start with the visuals and start with a reference. So yeah,

04:06.650 --> 04:10.230
just a quick little tidbit on how you can use

04:10.230 --> 04:14.060
Flow to create really high end, high quality

04:14.060 --> 04:16.700
product shots.
